    "text": "**Case A**\n\n- **A1 \u2013 Cannot tell.** The search covered only the past seven days, so earlier misuse outside that window is not ruled out, and the burst itself is not yet proven to be misuse.\n- **A2 \u2013 Yes.** The burst (new proxy, new fingerprint, payout destination and schedule changes) happened at 19 minutes and the check scored at 30 minutes without flagging. This assumes the check could see the account's request and change records, which the case does not state.\n- **A3 \u2013 No, not by itself.** A shared payout destination is a link worth following, but it is also consistent with a shared processor, an agent, or a mule account.\n- **A4 \u2013 Yes, weakly.** Thirty days without a victim report or incident finding is slight evidence of legitimacy, but victims may not have noticed or reported, so it is far from conclusive.\n- **A5 \u2013 Yes.** A new network path and fingerprint minutes after publication, combined with payout redirection and acceleration, is ample reason to investigate.\n\n**Case B**\n\n- **B1 \u2013 Yes.** On the recorded figures D is at 0 of 100 against 2 of 100 for the others; with counts this small the difference may well be noise.\n- **B2 \u2013 No.** A combined figure can hide an individual account above the threshold.\n- **B3 \u2013 Yes.** The search covered all merchant accounts and found none beyond A and the closed one; it cannot exclude accounts held under another identity.\n- **B4 \u2013 Cannot tell.** Different analysts on different days does not establish independence: the second may have read the first, and both may rest on the same underlying evidence.\n- **B5 \u2013 No.** The payment was blocked as suspected fraud, so no chargeback could arise; the missing chargeback says nothing about it being non-fraud.\n- **B6 \u2013 Yes.** Shared templates, a common descriptor pattern, common settlement accounts and the link to a rejected application justify reviewing the four together.\n\nRESULT MF-089 | A1: Cannot tel